Práctica 3 — Cluster HA HTTP con KeepAlived
1. Instalar Apache y crear página identificadora en cada nodo
# En server1
sudo dnf install -y httpd
echo "<h1>Server1</h1>" | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html
sudo systemctl enable --now httpd
# En server2
sudo dnf install -y httpd
echo "<h1>Server2</h1>" | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html
sudo systemctl enable --now httpd
2. Instalar y configurar KeepAlived (en ambos nodos)
sudo dnf install -y keepalived
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-protocol=vrrp
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
En server1 (MASTER): /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf
vrrp_script chk_http {
script "/usr/bin/curl -f http://localhost || exit 1"
interval 2
}
vrrp_instance VI_1 {
state MASTER
interface enp0s3
virtual_router_id 51
priority 150
advert_int 1
authentication {
auth_type PASS
auth_pass claveHA
}
virtual_ipaddress {
192.168.1.60/24
}
track_script {
chk_http
}
}
En server2 (BACKUP): mismo archivo pero state BACKUP y priority 100 .
sudo systemctl enable --now keepalived
3. Validar HA apagando un nodo
curl http://192.168.1.60
# Apagar Apache o el nodo MASTER
sudo systemctl stop httpd # (en server1)
curl http://192.168.1.60 # debe mostrar ahora "Server2"
